  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"With the self-published work 1975, Darboven first turned to literature as source for her transcriptions, filling her pages with excerpts from Baudelaire, Heine, Sartre, and Lawrence Weiner (in an edition of 100 copies). One year later, with the present artist book-titled by her arithmetical mantra (2=1,2; 1 + 1 =1,2; e.t.c.) via hand-stamp to the front cover-Darboven expanded-upon the work of the previous year; quite literally accounting for the passage of time through the addition of volume (this hefty book being a container of 240 leaves, printed rectos-only; versus the 80 leaves of 1975). The extra content was almost exclusively related to Jean-Paul Sartre, who had celebrated his 70th birthday in 1975 (and who Darboven would honour with a related installation-For Jean-Paul Sartre-at Leo Castelli in 1976). In addition to performing her checksum operations on Sartre's birthdates, Darboven chose passages from his autobiography Les mots, along with an interview with Michel Contat (as translated &amp; published by Der Spiegel); insofar as both of those sources addressed the relationship between writing, thinking, and reading, and (as per Bippus): a new autobiographical writing... in which the authors disappear between the lines of their text... (cf. Seeing, of course, is also an art': Writing-reading as an aesthetic labor of mediation...). Also used as source for this extra volume: an interview with Simone de Beauvoir (also published in Der Spiegel, 1976: Das Ewig Weibliche ist eine Lüge). -- Jason Rovito documentation</subfield>
  </datafield>
